\chapter{Networks, HTTP, IPC, and cryptographic adapters}
\label{ch:59}
\index{Networks, HTTP, IPC, and cryptographic adapters}

The library includes bounded socket and network helpers, HTTP modules with native companions, digest operations, and several messaging facades. Read the exact provider's limits. The small numeric-IPv4 socket bridge and the HTTP client are different interfaces. The in-process IPC mailbox is distinct from process supervision and framed kernel IPC. The \OnxCodeTerm{nng} module name does not imply linkage with upstream libnng.

\section{Test data paths independently}
Use loopback or fixture-based HTTP tests so response handling does not depend on a live service. Separate peer-close or EOF from zero-length data and from an operation error. Check digests against known-answer inputs, including an empty input and the documented encoding of a nonempty result. The checked source signature and exact bridge layout describe how to pass values. Secure deployment properties such as transport verification and key lifecycle need evidence in the chosen provider and surrounding application. Native network kernel families appear in \cref{ch:103}.

\section{Two meanings of sending a message}
Trace \OnxCodeTerm{network\_send\_all} with a five-byte payload and suppose the adapter reports progress of two bytes, then three bytes. The first call uses offset zero into the original string. The next call must use two, not zero, and both calls refer to the original payload. After the second result the offset equals the length, so the function can report success. If the second call instead returned four, it would be claiming more progress than the three remaining bytes; the helper rejects this as well as a nonpositive return. A failure after partial progress does not imply that nothing was sent. Retrying the whole payload blindly could duplicate its prefix.

For an empty string the loop takes no iterations and returns true. That observation tests the loop's empty-input behavior, not connectivity to a peer. For a binary response use the socket hex-receive wrapper when arbitrary bytes are possible; a text receive can consume bytes and then report that they contain NUL. Trying a hex read afterwards cannot retrieve those already consumed bytes.

Now compare \OnxCodeTerm{ipc\_send} on the in-process mailbox. It prepends a message and returns a new list. Sending A and then B using the successive returned lists and receiving once gives B and the remaining list containing A. There is no socket offset, partial write, or implicit FIFO delivery in that calculation. Keeping the first list also preserves an immutable snapshot of the earlier mailbox.

An empty mailbox also has its own contract: the receive helper fails on it. Use \OnxCodeTerm{ipc\_has\_message} before taking the head when emptiness is ordinary application flow, rather than interpreting it as a network EOF.

